<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theravada Buddhism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Theravada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theravada <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> is the branch of Buddhism that relies on the Pali canon and became dominant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Its spread in the later period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> emphasised monastic communities and individual merit. The rise of Theravada Buddhism reduced the emphasis on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-kingship">divine kingship</a> and large-scale temple construction, altering the state's ideological basis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism.jpg" alt="Theravada Buddhism" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: เทวประภาส มากคล้าย · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Khung_Taphao_salakabhatta_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new state drew on earlier traditions: Khmer court ritual and administrative vocabulary from Lavo, <b>Theravada Buddhism</b> shared with Sukhothai to the north, and the commercial connections of the lower Chao Phraya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Society remained mainly rural and Buddhist, with Confucian traditions in Vietnam, <b>Theravada Buddhism</b> in Laos and Cambodia, and growing Catholic and new religious communities, among them the Cao Dai, founded in 1926.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Law combined customary norms with religious principles derived from Hinduism and later Mahayana and <b>Theravada Buddhism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The language of the region, Magadhi Prakrit, was later associated with the Buddha's own speech, and Pali, the language of the <b>Theravada</b> canon, was long called Magadhi in Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Theravada Buddhism</b> was central to social and political life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Lao tradition credits Fa Ngum and his Khmer queen with bringing <b>Theravada Buddhism</b> and Cambodian monks to the court, together with the gold Buddha image known as the Phra Bang, although the image probably reached the capital later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
